<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ganden Phodrang</h1><p class="st-meta">1642 CE – 1912 CE · capital Lhasa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The government of the Dalai Lamas, founded in 1642, which ruled Tibet from Lhasa and the Potala Palace for three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1642 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khoshut-khanate">Khoshut Khanate</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tsangpa-dynasty">Tsangpa dynasty</a> and conferred authority over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a>, Ngawang Lobsang Gyatso. The government took its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama's</a> residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drepung-monastery">Drepung</a> monastery and soon moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a>, built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> from 1645.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> was governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a> or their regents with a council of ministers (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashag">Kashag</a>, formalised in 1751) and a dual service of monk and lay officials. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school dominated, and monasteries held vast estates. After intervening against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> in 1720, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> stationed ambans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-urn">Golden Urn</a> in 1793, though their real authority fluctuated.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The British expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-younghusband">Younghusband</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> in 1904, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> troops occupied it in 1910, driving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">Thirteenth Dalai Lama</a> into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. After the Qing fell, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> expelled the Chinese garrison in 1912, and the same government continued as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> (1912-1951).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ganden-phodrang.jpg" alt="Ganden Phodrang" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: René Heise · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lhasa_Potala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1642 CE – 1912 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lhasa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fifth Dalai Lama, Sangye Gyatso (regent), Thirteenth Dalai Lama</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism (Gelug)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ganden Phodrang government, Dalai Lama government</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1777&amp;focus=ganden-phodrang" aria-label="Ganden Phodrang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7052 1095 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ganden Phodrang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7372 1351L7372 1351M7372 1351L7567 1068M7372 1351L7371 1350"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7372 1351h0M7567 1068h0M7371 1350h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7372 1351h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7372 1351h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lhasa. <a href="/atlas?year=1777&amp;